WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.076 --> 00:00:15.160
Late the following afternoon, Mr.

2
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.661
Havisham came for Cedric in the earl's carriage.

3
00:00:19.961 --> 00:00:21.722
Cedric was sad to leave his mother.

4
00:00:24.903 --> 00:00:28.944
But when he went outside, he was immediately fascinated by the carriage.

5
00:00:31.224 --> 00:00:33.745
"Did you ever see such beautiful horses?"

6
00:00:34.265 --> 00:00:35.486
Cedric asked Mr.

7
00:00:35.506 --> 00:00:43.189
Havisham. "The earl's stable is full of magnificent horses," the lawyer replied.

8
00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.291
Cedric squinted at a design on the door.

9
00:00:47.311 --> 00:00:53.313
"That little crown with the big D above it—is that a coronet?"

10
00:00:53.333 --> 00:01:02.445
"Yes, and the D stands for Dorincourt."

11
00:01:02.465 --> 00:01:05.807
"Oh, that's so people realize the earl is inside."

12
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:08.008
Cedric looked at Mr.

13
00:01:08.028 --> 00:01:12.131
Havisham. "Or somebody the earl knows—like you."

14
00:01:12.151 --> 00:01:14.272
"Or you."

15
00:01:15.413 --> 00:01:17.695
Mr. Havisham gave a curt nod.

16
00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:23.258
A man in uniform held open the door.

17
00:01:23.278 --> 00:01:26.721
"Mr. Havisham, who's that?"

18
00:01:27.281 --> 00:01:28.142
Cedric whispered.

19
00:01:28.162 --> 00:01:31.464
"A servant called a footman.

20
00:01:32.456 --> 00:01:34.498
There are more footmen at the castle.

21
00:01:35.819 --> 00:01:37.141
Now hurry up and get in.

22
00:01:38.022 --> 00:01:42.246
You don't want to be late for your first meeting with your grandfather."

23
00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.974
It took only a few minutes to reach the gate of the great park surrounding the castle.

24
00:01:53.016 --> 00:01:57.100
Cedric peered out the window at a pair of stone lions guarding the entrance.

25
00:01:59.796 --> 00:02:02.778
A woman and two boys came out of an ivy-covered cottage.

26
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.923
While the mother opened the gate, the boys waved to Cedric.

27
00:02:10.684 --> 00:02:13.246
Then their mother curtseyed and smiled at him.

28
00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:19.991
"I don't know those people," Cedric said, "but they act like they know me."

29
00:02:20.011 --> 00:02:24.875
"They know you're Lord Fauntleroy," Mr.

30
00:02:24.915 --> 00:02:26.036
Havisham explained.

31
00:02:28.136 --> 00:02:30.137
Cedric took off his cap and waved.

32
00:02:30.157 --> 00:02:32.438
"Good afternoon!"

33
00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:33.559
he called out.

34
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.961
The woman's smile grew even broader.

35
00:02:37.981 --> 00:02:40.342
"Welcome, your lordship!"

36
00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.365
Cedric turned to Mr.

37
00:02:46.385 --> 00:02:50.106
Havisham. "That woman looks as if she likes children.

38
00:02:51.227 --> 00:02:53.548
I'd enjoy coming here to play with her sons."

39
00:02:55.316 --> 00:03:00.137
Mr. Havisham didn't tell him that a young lord doesn't play with a gatekeeper's children.

40
00:03:01.458 --> 00:03:04.899
There was plenty of time for Cedric to learn the rules of his new life.

41
00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:10.681
The carriage rolled down a broad avenue with tall trees on either side.

42
00:03:12.161 --> 00:03:14.702
It passed masses of ferns and flowers.

43
00:03:16.842 --> 00:03:19.183
Cedric pointed excitedly out the window.

44
00:03:19.203 --> 00:03:22.024
"There's a whole herd of deer!

45
00:03:25.216 --> 00:03:26.457
Who do they belong to?"

46
00:03:28.018 --> 00:03:30.259
Mr. Havisham smiled at Cedric's delight.

47
00:03:30.279 --> 00:03:34.641
"The earl—and one day, you."

48
00:03:34.661 --> 00:03:37.443
"What would Mr.

49
00:03:37.503 --> 00:03:38.743
Hobbs think of that?"

50
00:03:39.244 --> 00:03:39.944
Cedric said.

51
00:03:39.964 --> 00:03:44.146
"This park is bigger than Central Park in New York.

52
00:03:45.367 --> 00:03:46.988
But where's the castle?"

53
00:03:48.289 --> 00:03:49.830
"Several miles away."

54
00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.872
Cedric settled back against the seat cushions.

55
00:03:54.904 --> 00:03:58.067
"That's a long way for somebody to live from his front gate."

56
00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:07.494
As the castle came into view, the setting sun turned its windows to gold.

57
00:04:07.514 --> 00:04:12.878
"Ohh." Cedric sighed.

58
00:04:12.898 --> 00:04:17.402
"It's just like a king's palace in a fairy tale."

59
00:04:20.905 --> 00:04:24.448
Inside the castle a line of servants was waiting to greet Cedric.

60
00:04:26.556 --> 00:04:29.118
At the front of the line stood a kind-looking woman.

61
00:04:29.138 --> 00:04:33.701
"Lord Fauntleroy," Mr.

62
00:04:33.741 --> 00:04:36.763
Havisham said, "this is Mrs.

63
00:04:36.883 --> 00:04:38.584
Mellon, the housekeeper."

64
00:04:40.245 --> 00:04:41.626
Cedric's eyes lit up.

65
00:04:41.646 --> 00:04:44.828
"Thank you for giving the cat to my mother."

66
00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:47.730
Mrs. Mellon looked pleased.

67
00:04:48.711 --> 00:04:51.873
"The cat left two fine kittens here.

68
00:04:53.034 --> 00:04:55.576
I'll send them up to your lordship's rooms."

69
00:04:57.156 --> 00:05:00.018
Mr. Havisham said something to her in a low voice.

70
00:05:00.038 --> 00:05:03.761
"In the library, sir," she replied.

71
00:05:03.781 --> 00:05:08.105
"I've been instructed that his lordship should enter the room alone."

72
00:05:11.507 --> 00:05:15.911
Cedric looked around with great interest as a footman escorted him to the library.

73
00:05:18.392 --> 00:05:22.696
The footman opened the door and announced, "Lord Fauntleroy."

74
00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.523
Cedric entered a splendid room filled with books and heavy furniture.

75
00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:34.666
The room was so dark that Cedric thought no one was there.

76
00:05:36.287 --> 00:05:39.169
Then he saw someone sitting in the easy chair by the fire.

77
00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.051
The man didn't bother to look up.

78
00:05:43.852 --> 00:05:48.055
But the huge dog lying by the fire noticed the boy and walked over to him.

79
00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:54.500
The man in the chair spoke to the dog.

80
00:05:55.616 --> 00:05:56.556
"Come, Dougal."

81
00:05:58.997 --> 00:06:01.058
Cedric put his hand on the dog's collar.

82
00:06:02.198 --> 00:06:03.958
Together they approached the man.

83
00:06:03.978 --> 00:06:07.879
"Are you the earl?

84
00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:10.400
I'm your grandson, Cedric.

85
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.461
Mr. Havisham brought me from New York."

86
00:06:15.001 --> 00:06:16.422
Cedric held out his hand.

87
00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:18.362
"I hope you're well.

88
00:06:19.262 --> 00:06:20.623
I'm glad to meet you."

89
00:06:22.303 --> 00:06:24.804
The earl was surprised by Cedric's good manners.

90
00:06:26.044 --> 00:06:30.785
It pleased the grim, old nobleman that Cedric wasn't afraid of the dog or himself.

91
00:06:30.805 --> 00:06:34.447
"Glad to meet me, are you?"

92
00:06:35.047 --> 00:06:36.787
the earl said as they shook hands.

93
00:06:36.807 --> 00:06:40.408
"Of course," Cedric replied.

94
00:06:40.428 --> 00:06:43.449
"Everyone is glad to meet their relatives."

95
00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.290
The earl thought about his own family.

96
00:06:48.571 --> 00:06:52.612
He'd spent his life quarreling with his relatives and throwing them out of his house.

97
00:06:54.644 --> 00:06:56.045
They all despised him.

98
00:06:57.585 --> 00:07:02.167
In fact, 35 years had passed since he'd last seen his only sister.

99
00:07:03.607 --> 00:07:05.188
Cedric took a seat near the earl.

100
00:07:05.208 --> 00:07:11.630
"Any boy would love his grandfather, especially one who's been so kind."

101
00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:13.851
The earl was startled.

102
00:07:13.871 --> 00:07:15.731
"What do you mean?"

103
00:07:16.172 --> 00:07:16.832
he demanded.
